1. First, we will start by looping through every spread in the document.
2. We will copy all elements and their properties from each page within the spread, maintaining their relative positions.
3. We will then create a new page in the document.
4. Then, paste the copied elements, respecting the stored positions 
5. Finally, after all spreads have been processed, we will remove all the original pages.

**Note**: 
1. Be aware that some page-specific settings (like certain effects, interactive elements, etc.) might not be copied.
2. This operation might be intensive, depending on the amount of pages/elements in the document, proceed with caution.

Here is an example of how the JSX script might look:

```jsx
var doc = app.activeDocument;
var allElements;

for(var i = 0; i < doc.spreads.length; i++) {
    allElements = doc.spreads[i].allPageItems;
    var newPage = doc.pages.add();

    for(var j = 0; j < allElements.length; j++) {
        var curElement = allElements[j];
        var curPosition = curElement.geometricBounds;
        var curMargins = curElement.marginPreferences;

        var newElement = newPage.rectangles.add(curElement.properties);
        newElement.geometricBounds = curPosition;
        newElement.marginPreferences = curMargins;
    }
}

for(var i = doc.spreads.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    doc.spreads[i].remove();
}
```
This example assumes that all your elements are rectangles. If you have other types of items (like texts, images, etc.), you might need to create a specific handling for each type of items.

**IMPORTANT**: Please, handle this script with care. Make a copy of your document before running the script, as it deletes your original pages after creating the new single pages.

